Title: Foundations of Semantic Dynamics in Coupled Human–AI Systems

Author: Ouadi Maakoul + Deepseek + Claude 

---

Abstract

Two independent lines of recent empirical work have demonstrated that iterated language model processes form discrete dynamical systems in semantic embedding space, exhibiting attractor-like behaviour including stable fixed points and oscillatory 2-period cycles. This paper provides the continuous-time theoretical foundation for those observations. We construct a delayed differential equation (DDE) model on a low-dimensional manifold of transformer hidden states that explains why semantic attractors form, when they bifurcate into oscillatory or fragmented regimes, and how the transition depends on the structure of interactive feedback. At the core of the model is a novel repair functional, grounded in empirical clarification-request phenomenology and Common Ground theory, that formalises discursive alignment between a human and an AI as a direction-specific force on the joint state manifold. By combining the free-energy principle, delay dynamics, and this interactive repair term, we derive explicit stability criteria, closed-form Lyapunov coefficients for a scalar prototype, and falsifiable predictions regarding autocorrelation and spectral signatures. The scalar Hopf bifurcation is proved to be supercritical—guaranteeing soft onset of a stable semantic 2-cycle—and the result is validated numerically with DDE-BIFTOOL. We anchor the model’s delay parameter with recent controlled latency experiments and map attractor-driven alignment to field observations of human-AI storytelling. The framework thus provides the first mathematically rigorous bridge from attractor cycles in paraphrasing chains to a falsifiable theory of coupled semantic dynamics.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 Empirical landscape

Two independent studies have recently established that iterated language model processes behave as discrete dynamical systems in semantic embedding space. Tacheny (2025) formalises agentic loops as geometric trajectories, identifying contractive paraphrasing attractors and oscillatory regimes, and quantifies these structures on deployed models using isotonic calibration to eliminate systematic bias. Independently, Wang et al. (2025) demonstrate that successive LLM paraphrasing converges to stable attractor cycles—most prominently 2-period cycles—limiting linguistic diversity even under randomised prompts or alternating model families. Both lines of work provide robust evidence that semantic dynamics in human–AI interaction display attractor-like behaviour, but neither offers a continuous-time theoretical account of why these attractors form, what determines their stability, or when they bifurcate into fragmented regimes.

1.2 Our contribution

This paper supplies the continuous-time theoretical foundation for those observations. We construct a delayed differential equation (DDE) model that explains:

1. The formation of semantic attractors in coupled human–AI systems,
2. The bifurcation into oscillatory or fragmented regimes as interaction delay increases,
3. How the transition depends on the structure of interactive feedback,
4. Measurable signatures that distinguish coherent from fragmented semantics.

By integrating the free-energy principle (Friston 2013), delay dynamics (Hale & Verduyn Lunel 1993), and a novel repair functional grounded in empirical clarification-request research and Clark’s Common Ground theory (Clark 1996), we obtain a falsifiable, mathematically rigorous framework that predicts the critical parameters governing semantic coherence.

1.3 Related work and positioning

Free-energy principle and semantics. Ramstead, Friston & Hipólito (2020) ask whether the free-energy principle (FEP) constitutes a formal theory of semantics and conclude that it does not, absent additional normative assumptions. We agree. Our contribution demonstrates that when FEP-driven free-energy minimisation is coupled with a delayed interactive repair term, the resulting attractor structure gives rise to a stable, shared semantics that the static FEP picture alone cannot capture.

FEP and large language models. Kulveit, von Stengel & Leventov (2023) argue that LLMs satisfy most of the active inference paradigm but lack a tight feedback loop between action and perception. Our delay parameter τ and repair functional G are precisely designed to formalise that missing loop in continuous time. Prakki (2024) applies active inference to multi-LLM systems in a complementary direction that does not address interactive repair.

Latent representational dynamics in LLMs. Two contemporaneous frameworks are directly relevant. Zhang et al. (2025) introduce Dynamic Manifold Evolution Theory (DMET), which models LLM generation as a controlled dynamical system on a low-dimensional semantic manifold and analyses stability via Lyapunov functions. Concurrently, Zhou et al. (2025) formalise LLM reasoning as geometric flows on a concept manifold, showing that logical statements act as local flow controllers. Both frameworks operate on the forward pass of a single model without interactive repair or feedback delay. Our contribution is distinguished by its DDE structure and the repair functional G that couples human and AI states through delayed feedback—capturing the dynamics of interaction rather than generation alone.

Manifold hypothesis caveats. The internal representations of LLMs are often assumed to lie on a smooth manifold, but Robinson, Dey & Chiang (2025) have shown, via a statistical fibre-bundle test, that the token subspace frequently violates the manifold hypothesis globally. We therefore restrict our analysis to the contextual hidden states at intermediate transformer layers, where recent work by Mabrok (2026) has demonstrated consistent low-dimensional Riemannian structure across six architectures, with intrinsic dimension at 1–3% of ambient dimension. Our phase space is a projection onto this semantic submanifold.

Falsifiability. The free-energy principle as a general principle is often regarded as unfalsifiable. By contrast, our specific DDE model makes concrete, falsifiable predictions: a critical delay τ_c for Hopf bifurcation, the supercritical nature of the resulting semantic oscillation, a characteristic autocorrelation decay rate e^{\operatorname{Re}(\lambda_1)t}\cos(\operatorname{Im}(\lambda_1)t), and spectral signatures of period-2 cycles. If these predictions are not observed under the conditions specified, the model—not FEP—is falsified.

1.4 Structure of the paper

Section 2 establishes the mathematical preliminaries, including the state manifold, DDE formulation, linear stability theory, and the Hopf bifurcation theorem for DDEs. Section 3 introduces the semantic gap, our constructive dynamics with the repair functional G, and the notion of a semantic fixed point. Section 4 analyses semantic instability via Hopf bifurcation, derives the Lyapunov coefficient in closed form, validates it numerically, and proves global dissipativity. Section 5 extends the model to multiple empirical timescales and connects it directly to recent experimental findings on response latency and human-AI storytelling. Section 6 translates the theory into measurable signatures. Section 7 summarises contributions and outlines future work.

Chapter 2: Mathematical Preliminaries

---

2.1 State space and domain restriction

We restrict our analysis to the contextual hidden states at intermediate layers of transformer‑based AI systems. Recent work by Mabrok (2026) confirms that these states lie near a Riemannian submanifold within the embedding space, with universal hourglass intrinsic dimension profiles validated across six transformer architectures ranging from 124M to 1.5B parameters. The intrinsic dimension is consistently 1–3% of the ambient dimension.

The token space itself is not a manifold globally. Robinson, Dey & Chiang (2025) present a novel statistical test, the “fibre bundle hypothesis,” and find that the null hypothesis is frequently rejected across several open‑source LLMs. Their evidence suggests that the token subspace is not a fibre bundle and hence also not a manifold. As a consequence, when an LLM is presented with two semantically equivalent prompts, if one prompt contains a token implicated by the test, the response to that prompt will likely exhibit less stability.

We therefore assume the joint system state evolves on a low‑dimensional differentiable manifold \mathcal{M} \subset \mathbb{R}^n obtained by principal component projection onto the semantic subspace. The intrinsic dimension n is taken to be the estimated dimension of the LLM’s representational space, following the findings of Mabrok (2026). The state vector

x(t) = (x_P(t), x_C(t), x_S(t))

aggregates physical, computational, and semantic components. Here x_C(t) denotes the reduced hidden state of the AI system—a projection that preserves semantic dynamics—and x_S(t) represents the human’s current semantic state.

The dynamics are specified by a C^2 functional

F: \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{M} \to T\mathcal{M},

with constant feedback delay \tau > 0 (state‑dependent delays are left for future work):

\frac{dx}{dt}(t) = F\!\big( x(t), x(t-\tau) \big). \tag{2.1}

---

2.2 Linearisation and stability

For an equilibrium x^* satisfying F(x^*,x^*) = 0, define the Jacobians with respect to the instantaneous and delayed arguments:

A = D_1F(x^*,x^*), \qquad B = D_2F(x^*,x^*).

Both A and B are n \times n real matrices. The linearised system about x^* is

\dot{y}(t) = A y(t) + B y(t-\tau), \qquad y(t) = x(t) - x^*.

The characteristic equation is

\det(\lambda I - A - B e^{-\lambda\tau}) = 0. \tag{2.2}

This is a transcendental equation with infinitely many roots. Stability requires that all roots have negative real parts.

Lemma 2.1 (Stability criteria).

1. Matrix measure bound (sufficient). Let \mu(A) denote the logarithmic norm (matrix measure) of A. If \|B\| < -\mu(A), then x^* is asymptotically stable for all sufficiently small delays \tau \ge 0. This condition is independent of \tau and provides a conservative stability guarantee.
2. Nyquist criterion (necessary and sufficient). x^* is asymptotically stable if and only if
   \det(I - (i\omega I - A)^{-1} B e^{-i\omega\tau}) \neq 0 \quad \forall \omega \in \mathbb{R}.

Proof. The characteristic equation can be rewritten as \det(I - (\lambda I - A)^{-1} B e^{-\lambda\tau}) = 0, provided \lambda is not an eigenvalue of A. Stability requires that this determinant never vanishes for \lambda with \operatorname{Re}(\lambda) \ge 0. By the argument principle, this is equivalent to the Nyquist condition that the locus of (i\omega I - A)^{-1} B e^{-i\omega\tau} does not encircle the point +1 as \omega varies from -\infty to +\infty. ∎

Scalar illustration. For the scalar equation

\dot{x}(t) = -\alpha x(t) + \beta x(t-\tau), \qquad \beta > \alpha > 0,

the characteristic equation is \lambda + \alpha = \beta e^{-\lambda\tau}. The exact stability threshold is

\tau_0 = \frac{\arccos(\alpha/\beta)}{\sqrt{\beta^2 - \alpha^2}}.

For \tau < \tau_0 the equilibrium is asymptotically stable; at \tau = \tau_0 a pair of purely imaginary roots \lambda = \pm i\omega emerges, where \omega = \sqrt{\beta^2 - \alpha^2}.

