2.3 Hopf bifurcation theorem for delay differential equations

We state the theorem following the formulation of Hale & Verduyn Lunel (1993, Chapter 11).

Theorem 2.2 (Hopf bifurcation for DDEs). Consider the system (2.1) with parameter \tau. Assume:

(H1) F is C^3 in a neighbourhood of (x^*, x^*).
(H2) At \tau = \tau_c, the characteristic equation has a simple pair of purely imaginary roots \lambda = \pm i\omega_c, \omega_c > 0, and no other roots on the imaginary axis.
(H3) Transversality: \operatorname{Re}\!\big(\frac{d\lambda}{d\tau}\big)\big|_{\tau = \tau_c} \neq 0.

Then a Hopf bifurcation occurs at \tau = \tau_c: a unique branch of small‑amplitude periodic solutions bifurcates from x^*. The direction and stability are determined by the first Lyapunov coefficient l_1, computed via centre‑manifold reduction following Faria & Magalhães (1995). If l_1 < 0, the bifurcation is supercritical (stable limit cycle for \tau > \tau_c). If l_1 > 0, it is subcritical (unstable cycle, hysteresis, possible catastrophic fragmentation).

