Chapter 3: The Semantic Gap and the Dynamical Alternative

---

3.1 Static mapping limitations

Definition 3.1 (Static mapping limitation). A static mapping \Phi_{\text{static}} : \mathcal{M} \to \mathcal{S} would assign a fixed semantic label to each instantaneous state. Empirical evidence shows that such mappings suffer from fundamental barriers.

Arle (2026) demonstrates this with a minimal 4‑bit model (“ThoughtBox”) consisting of a ring of binary cells updated by a parity‑flip rule, coupled with a finite lookup table assigning conceptual tags to selected microstates. Two core failure modes are identified: (i) noise pushes the system into out‑of‑training‑set states that a semantic decoder cannot label (“missing‑label” errors); (ii) distinct microstates collapse into the same semantic tag (“many‑to‑one” grouping), obscuring their unique identities. These findings demonstrate inherent opacity in semantic mapping and suggest fundamental barriers to reverse‑engineering high‑level content.

These barriers motivate replacing \Phi_{\text{static}} with a dynamical notion of semantics.

---

3.2 Constructive dynamics

Let V(x) denote the variational free energy (Friston 2013). The full dynamics decompose as

F(x, x_\tau) = -\nabla V(x) \;+\; G(x, x_\tau) \;+\; H(x), \tag{3.1}

where:

· -\nabla V(x) drives the system towards free‑energy minima, encoding the internal generative model;
· G(x, x_\tau) formalises discursive repair between human and AI;
· H(x) encodes world coupling (sensorimotor/experimental feedback). In the conversational settings studied, H(x) is assumed negligible; its role becomes relevant when the interaction includes sensorimotor or experimental feedback loops, which may alter the bifurcation structure.

Repair functional G. Motivated by the clarification‑request phenomenology documented in Madureira & Schlangen (2023)—who annotated 8.8k Instruction Clarification Requests in the CoDraw dataset—and grounding it in Clark’s Common Ground theory (Clark 1996), we propose:

G(x, x_\tau) = \gamma\, \big[ x_S(t) - d_{\text{AI}}(x_C(t-\tau)) \big] \,K(x_P)\, \mathbf{v}, \qquad \gamma > 0.

Here:

· d_{\text{AI}} : \mathcal{M}_C \to \mathcal{M}_S is the AI’s decoder, assumed Lipschitz with constant L_d;
· K : \mathcal{M}_P \to [0,1] is a bounded attention kernel modulating the strength of repair;
· \mathbf{v} \in T\mathcal{M} is the unit direction vector of repair on the tangent bundle, ensuring G maps into T\mathcal{M}. In the minimal model, repair acts directly on the computational state x_C, i.e., \mathbf{v} = (0, \mathbf{u}, 0) with \mathbf{u} a unit vector.

The error term x_S(t) - d_{\text{AI}}(x_C(t-\tau)) captures the momentary misalignment between the human’s current semantic state and what the AI’s delayed response implied—a continuous‑time model of grounding repair. This functional form is not directly extracted from Madureira & Schlangen but is structurally motivated by their findings on how clarification requests serve as repair mechanisms when instructions are ambiguous or underspecified.

