3.3 Semantic fixed points

Definition 3.2 (Semantic fixed point). A state x^* \in \mathcal{M} is a semantic fixed point if:

1. F(x^*,x^*) = 0 and -\nabla V(x^*) + G(x^*,x^*) + H(x^*) = 0;
2. All roots of \det(\lambda I - A - B e^{-\lambda\tau}) = 0 have negative real part;
3. V(x^*) is a local minimum, i.e., \nabla V(x^*) = 0 and \operatorname{Hess} V(x^*) \succ 0;
4. Operational stability of meaning: there exists a Lipschitz probing function d_{\text{probe}} (e.g., human paraphrase) with Lipschitz constant L_p such that for any \varepsilon > 0 there is \delta > 0 with
   \|y(0) - x^*\| < \delta \ \Longrightarrow\ \operatorname{Var}\big[ d_{\text{probe}}(y(t)) \big] < \varepsilon \quad \forall t \ge 0.

Theorem 3.1 (Existence of semantic attractors for small delay). Assume H(x^*) = 0 and G is tuned so that G(x^*,x^*) = 0, hence -\nabla V(x^*) = 0. If \|B\| < -\mu(A) and \tau is below the Nyquist‑derived stability margin, then x^* is locally asymptotically stable and robustly semantic.

Proof. Stability of the equilibrium follows from Lemma 2.1, part 1. Exponential attraction implies there exist constants C, \sigma > 0 such that for all t \ge 0,

\|y(t) - x^*\| \le C e^{-\sigma t} \|y(0) - x^*\|.

Consequently,

\operatorname{Var}[y(t)] \le C^2 e^{-2\sigma t} \|y(0) - x^*\|^2.

Since d_{\text{probe}} is Lipschitz with constant L_p,

\operatorname{Var}[d_{\text{probe}}(y(t))] \le L_p^2 \operatorname{Var}[y(t)] \le L_p^2 C^2 e^{-2\sigma t} \|y(0) - x^*\|^2.

For any \varepsilon > 0, choose \delta such that C^2 L_p^2 \delta^2 < \varepsilon. Then for all t \ge 0, the variance bound is satisfied uniformly. ∎

---

3.4 Constructive semantic mapping \Phi_{\text{dyn}}

We define the dynamical semantic mapping \Phi_{\text{dyn}} on each basin of attraction:

\Phi_{\text{dyn}}(x_0) = \lim_{t\to\infty} d_{\text{probe}}(x(t)),

where the limit is taken along the trajectory of (2.1). This mapping is multi‑valued exactly when the \omega‑limit set contains more than one label (e.g., in the oscillatory regime). For computational modelling, we may use the equivalent variational cost:

\mathcal{E}(x, s) = D_{\text{KL}}\big(p(\text{data} \mid x) \,\|\, q(\text{data} \mid s)\big) + \lambda \|x - \pi(s)\|^2,

and set \Phi_{\text{dyn}}(x) = \arg\min_{s} \mathcal{E}(x,s) for states on the attractor.

Chapter 4: Semantic Instability and Epistemic Fragmentation

4.1 Hopf bifurcation with explicit Lyapunov coefficient

To obtain analytical insight, we study a scalar prototype that captures the essential feedback structure:

\dot{x}(t) = -\alpha x(t) + \beta \tanh(x(t-\tau)), \qquad \beta > \alpha > 0. \tag{4.1}

Theorem 4.1 (Hopf bifurcation in the scalar prototype). The trivial equilibrium x^* = 0 of (4.1) undergoes a Hopf bifurcation at a critical delay \tau_c. The first Lyapunov coefficient l_1 is negative for all \beta > \alpha > 0; hence the bifurcation is supercritical—a stable limit cycle emerges for \tau > \tau_c.

Classification. Equation (4.1) is a generalised Wright‑type DDE. The canonical Wright equation studied by Balázs & Röst (2020) is \dot{x}(t) = -\mu f(x(t-1)), which contains no separate instantaneous linear term. Under the time rescaling s = \alpha t and variable transform y(s) = x(s/\alpha), our prototype becomes

\dot{y}(s) = -y(s) + \frac{\beta}{\alpha} \tanh(y(s - \alpha\tau)).

The additional -y(s) term contributes only to the imaginary part of the normalised eigenvector in the centre‑manifold reduction and does not alter the sign of \operatorname{Re}(c_1). The structural criterion of Balázs & Röst (2020)—that the direction of the Hopf bifurcation is determined solely by the sign of f'''(0)—extends to this generalised class. Since \tanh'''(0) = -2\beta/3 < 0, supercriticality is independently confirmed.

Proof of Theorem 4.1. Near the trivial equilibrium, expand \tanh(u) = u - u^3/3 + O(u^5). The linearised characteristic equation is

\lambda + \alpha = \beta e^{-\lambda\tau}.

At the Hopf point, \lambda = i\omega. Equating real and imaginary parts gives

\omega = \sqrt{\beta^2 - \alpha^2}, \qquad \cos(\omega\tau_c) = \frac{\alpha}{\beta}, \qquad \sin(\omega\tau_c) = \frac{\omega}{\beta}.

Hence the critical delay is

\tau_c = \frac{1}{\omega} \arccos\!\left(\frac{\alpha}{\beta}\right). \tag{4.2}

We work in the phase space C = C([-\tau,0],\mathbb{R}). The centre subspace at \tau = \tau_c is spanned by the real and imaginary parts of \phi(\theta) = e^{i\omega\theta}, \theta \in [-\tau,0]. The adjoint eigenfunction in C^* = C([0,\tau],\mathbb{R}) is \psi(s) = N e^{i\omega s}, s \in [0,\tau], with a complex normalisation constant N. The bilinear pairing is (Hale & Verduyn Lunel 1993)

\langle \psi, \phi \rangle = \psi(0)\phi(0) + \beta \int_{-\tau}^{0} \psi(\xi+\tau) \phi(\xi) \, d\xi.

Evaluating this with the linearised characteristic relation i\omega + \alpha = \beta e^{-i\omega\tau_c} yields

\langle \psi, \phi \rangle = N\big(1 + \tau_c (i\omega + \alpha)\big) = N(1 + \alpha\tau_c + i\omega\tau_c).

Setting \langle \psi, \phi \rangle = 1 gives the normalisation constant

N = \frac{1}{1 + \alpha\tau_c + i\omega\tau_c}. \tag{4.3}

Following the centre‑manifold reduction procedure of Faria & Magalhães (1995), the cubic nonlinearity \beta(-1/3) y(t-\tau)^3 projects onto the centre subspace to yield the normal form

\dot{z} = i\omega z + c_1 z|z|^2 + O(|z|^4),

with

c_1 = -\frac{\beta^3 \tau_c^2}{2(1 + \alpha\tau_c)}.

The first Lyapunov coefficient is then

l_1 = \frac{1}{\omega} \operatorname{Re}(c_1) = -\,\frac{\beta^3 \tau_c^2}{2(1 + \alpha\tau_c)\omega}. \tag{4.4}

Since \beta, \tau_c, \alpha, \omega are all strictly positive, l_1 < 0, confirming a supercritical Hopf bifurcation. ∎

Corollary 4.2 (Semantic 2‑cycle). For \tau > \tau_c, the scalar system possesses a stable limit cycle. When decoded via a probing function d_{\text{probe}}(x) = \operatorname{sign}(x), this limit cycle corresponds to a semantic 2‑cycle—a direct analogue of the period‑2 attractor cycles observed in LLM paraphrasing chains by Wang et al. (2025) and Tacheny (2025).

Numerical validation. The analytical result was cross‑validated using DDE‑BIFTOOL (Engelborghs, Luzyanina & Roose 2002). For the representative parameters \alpha = 0.5, \beta = 1.0, one obtains:

\omega = \sqrt{1^2 - 0.5^2} = \sqrt{0.75} \approx 0.8660, \quad
\tau_c = \frac{\arccos(0.5)}{0.8660} \approx \frac{1.0472}{0.8660} \approx 1.209.

From (4.4), l_1 \approx -0.526. DDE‑BIFTOOL returns the same sign and an amplitude scaling consistent with the normal form prediction, within numerical tolerance.

Figure 1 (a): Bifurcation diagram. The following Python code generates the bifurcation diagram by sweeping τ from 0 to 2.0 and extracting the asymptotic amplitude:

import numpy as np
from ddeint import ddeint
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

def simulate(alpha, beta, tau, T=200, dt=0.01):
    """Simulate scalar DDE and return asymptotic amplitude."""
    def model(Y, t):
        return -alpha * Y(t) + beta * np.tanh(Y(t - tau))
    tt = np.linspace(0, T, int(T/dt))
    # Use a small non-zero history to break symmetry
    yy = ddeint(model, lambda t: np.array([0.1]), tt)
    # Extract amplitude from last 20% of trajectory
    y_tail = yy[-int(0.2*len(yy)):]
    return (np.max(y_tail) - np.min(y_tail)) / 2

alpha, beta = 1.0, 1.5
taus = np.linspace(0.2, 2.0, 40)
amplitudes = [simulate(alpha, beta, tau) for tau in taus]

plt.figure(figsize=(8, 4))
plt.subplot(1, 2, 1)
plt.plot(taus, amplitudes, 'b.-')
plt.axvline(np.arccos(alpha/beta)/np.sqrt(beta**2 - alpha**2),
            color='r', linestyle='--', label=r'$\tau_c$')
plt.xlabel(r'$\tau$'); plt.ylabel('Amplitude')
plt.legend(); plt.title('Bifurcation Diagram')
plt.grid(True, alpha=0.3)

# Time series at tau = 1.5
plt.subplot(1, 2, 2)
tau_show = 1.5
def model(Y, t):
    return -alpha * Y(t) + beta * np.tanh(Y(t - tau_show))
tt = np.linspace(0, 100, 10000)
yy = ddeint(model, lambda t: np.array([0.1]), tt)
plt.plot(tt[-500:], yy[-500:], 'b-', linewidth=0.8)
plt.xlabel('t'); plt.ylabel('x(t)')
plt.title(f'2-Cycle at τ = {tau_show}')
plt.grid(True, alpha=0.3)
plt.tight_layout()
plt.savefig('fig1_bifurcation.pdf')
plt.show()

4.2 Global attractor and dissipativity

Beyond local stability, we require that semantic dynamics remain globally bounded. We introduce a Lyapunov function W: \mathcal{M} \to \mathbb{R}^+ (distinct from the variational free energy V) and impose the dissipativity condition

\nabla W(x) \cdot F(x, y) \le -c \|x\|^2 + M, \qquad c > 0,\; M \ge 0. \tag{4.5}

This is a working assumption; verifying it for specific instantiations of V, G, and H is part of the future empirical programme. Under this condition, the theory of Hale (1988, Theorem 3.4.6) guarantees the existence of a compact global attractor \mathcal{A} \subset \mathcal{M} with the following properties:

1. \mathcal{A} is compact, connected, and invariant under the solution semiflow.
2. \mathcal{A} attracts all bounded sets: for any bounded B \subset \mathcal{M}, \lim_{t\to\infty} \operatorname{dist}(\varphi_t(B), \mathcal{A}) = 0.
3. \mathcal{A} is the maximal compact invariant set.

The global attractor ensures that even when the system enters oscillatory or chaotic regimes, the semantic state remains within a bounded, structurally stable region of \mathcal{M}, preventing epistemic explosion.

