4.3 Connection to empirical attractor cycles

The stable 2‑cycle produced by the scalar prototype for \tau > \tau_c provides a mechanistic explanation for the period‑2 paraphrasing attractors documented by Wang et al. (2025) and the oscillatory regimes identified by Tacheny (2025). In our framework, the interaction delay \tau acts as the control parameter: when repair is sufficiently fast (small \tau), the human–AI dyad converges to a shared semantic fixed point; when repair is delayed beyond \tau_c, the system oscillates between two distinct semantic states, corresponding to a fragmentation of meaning. This prediction is directly testable by systematically varying response latency in human–AI interaction experiments, as initiated by Tan et al. (2026).

Chapter 5: Multiple Delays and Empirical Grounding

---

5.1 Multiple timescales

Real human–AI interaction involves several distinct delays: the AI’s response latency \tau_{\text{AI}}, the human’s comprehension and reflection time \tau_{\text{human}}, and, in broader settings, social or environmental latencies \tau_{\text{social}}. When multiple delays are present, the characteristic equation generalises from (2.2) to

\det\!\Big(\lambda I - A - \sum_{i} B_i e^{-\lambda \tau_i}\Big) = 0, \tag{5.1}

where each B_i is the Jacobian with respect to the state delayed by \tau_i. A sufficient condition for delay‑independent stability is the small‑gain condition

\sum_i \|B_i\| < -\mu(A). \tag{5.2}

Inequality (5.2) guarantees that no purely imaginary root can satisfy (5.1) for any combination of non‑negative delays, ensuring that the equilibrium remains asymptotically stable regardless of the specific latency values.

In the practically relevant case where the AI latency is much shorter than the human reflection time (\tau_{\text{AI}} \ll \tau_{\text{human}}), singular perturbation theory allows reduction to an effective single‑delay model. If the fast delayed term can be adiabatically eliminated by replacing it with its equilibrium contribution, the system behaves as if governed by a single effective delay. Furthermore, when the coupling strengths of the two feedback channels are comparable (\beta_{\text{AI}} \approx \beta_{\text{human}}), the effective delay approximates the sum

\tau_{\text{eff}} \approx \tau_{\text{AI}} + \tau_{\text{human}}. \tag{5.3}

This additive accumulation has an important consequence: even when each individual delay lies below the critical bifurcation threshold \tau_c, their combination can push the system past the bifurcation point, inducing semantic oscillations. If the coupling strengths are markedly different, the dominant delay is simply the one with the larger coefficient, and the additive rule requires modification; but for the conversational dyads we analyse, approximate parity of coupling is the natural regime, because both human comprehension and AI generation contribute with comparable weight to the repair dynamics.

Empirical anchoring of \tau. Tan et al. (2026) conducted controlled experiments in which participants performed taxonomy‑driven knowledge tasks (Creation and Advice) while interacting with an LLM whose response latency was fixed at 2 s, 9 s, or 20 s. Their log analyses revealed that, although user interaction behaviours were robust to latency, participants who experienced the 2‑second latency rated the LLM’s outputs as less thoughtful and useful than those who experienced 9‑ or 20‑second delays, suggesting that users partially attribute longer latencies to AI deliberation. At the highest latency (20 s), some participants shifted their interpretation toward frustration. These empirical values provide realistic ranges for \tau_{\text{AI}}.

For typical conversational settings with AI response latencies of 2–9 s, \tau_{\text{AI}} is plausibly well below the critical bifurcation threshold of the coupled system. However, under high‑latency conditions (\tau_{\text{AI}} \ge 20 s) or when the human’s reflection time adds significantly to the effective delay, \tau_{\text{eff}} can approach or exceed \tau_c. Our model therefore predicts a qualitative change in interaction dynamics—from convergent alignment to oscillatory fragmentation—as the combined feedback delay crosses the critical value.

