5.2 Empirical evidence for attractor‑driven alignment

Fundal et al. (2025) studied over three thousand museum visitors who co‑authored stories with an LLM in a turn‑taking setup over nine days. Using sentiment analysis, semantic embeddings, and information‑theoretic measures of novelty and resonance, they found that affective alignment was primarily model‑driven, with limited mutual convergence. Human participants explored a broader semantic space and introduced more novel, narratively influential contributions, while the AI tended to pull the interaction back toward its own attractors.

This pattern maps directly onto our theoretical picture. The model‑driven alignment with limited mutual convergence corresponds to the regime where the AI’s internal free‑energy gradient -\nabla V dominates the dynamics, while the interactive repair term G is too weak—either because the coupling strength \gamma is small or because the feedback delay \tau is sub‑critical but the repair direction \mathbf{v} does not sufficiently engage the human’s semantic state. In the language of Section 3.3, the system resides in the basin of attraction of a semantic fixed point that is largely determined by the AI’s generative model, with the human acting as an exploratory perturbation. The “exploratory” versus “contractive” regimes reported by Fundal et al. thus correspond to the pre‑ and post‑bifurcation dynamics of our DDE model: for small \tau the interaction converges contractively toward the AI’s attractor; for larger \tau the interactive repair loop destabilises that fixed point, allowing the human to drive the joint state through a richer semantic landscape—but at the risk of fragmentation.

Similarly, the attractor cycles documented by Tacheny (2025) and Wang et al. (2025) in purely LLM paraphrasing chains are special cases of our framework with the human state frozen (or absent) and the repair loop replaced by self‑feedback. The period‑2 cycles they observe are exactly the stable limit cycles predicted by Theorem 4.1 when the effective self‑feedback delay exceeds \tau_c.

---

5.3 Parameter regimes and bifurcation in real systems

To bridge the scalar prototype with real interaction data, we note that the critical delay \tau_c in (4.2) is expressed in the natural time units of the dynamics, which are set by the relaxation rates \alpha and \beta. In a conversational setting, \alpha^{-1} corresponds to the characteristic time over which an unresolved semantic discrepancy would decay under the AI’s internal dynamics alone, while \beta^{-1} sets the timescale for the delayed feedback. A plausible order‑of‑magnitude estimate, based on the typical duration of a conversational turn (a few seconds) and the observation that meaning negotiation often resolves within two to three turns, suggests that \alpha,\beta \sim 0.1–1 s⁻¹, placing \tau_c in the range of a few seconds to a few tens of seconds. This is consistent with the Tan et al. (2026) latencies: the short‑latency condition (2 s) is safely sub‑critical, the medium‑latency condition (9 s) may approach criticality for some parameter choices, and the long‑latency condition (20 s) could exceed \tau_c when combined with human reflection time. Systematic parameter estimation from interaction logs remains a task for future work.

---

Chapter 6: Connecting to Measurable Signatures

---

6.1 Autocorrelation structure near a stable fixed point

When the system operates in the sub‑critical regime (\tau < \tau_c), the dominant dynamics near the semantic fixed point are governed by the rightmost characteristic root \lambda_1. Linearising (2.1) about x^* and projecting onto the observable semantic embedding s(t) = d_{\text{probe}}(x(t)), we obtain the autocorrelation function

R(t) \equiv \langle s(t_0 + t), s(t_0) \rangle \sim e^{\operatorname{Re}(\lambda_1) t} \cos\!\big(\operatorname{Im}(\lambda_1) t\big) \qquad (t \to \infty), \tag{6.1}

where the average is taken over stationary fluctuations. The exponential decay rate \operatorname{Re}(\lambda_1) < 0 and the oscillation frequency \operatorname{Im}(\lambda_1) are direct outputs of the characteristic equation (2.2). Because d_{\text{probe}} is Lipschitz, the linearised output retains the same dominant time constants; nonlinear corrections become significant only for larger excursions and would appear as higher‑harmonic content or a shift in the effective decay rate.

Equation (6.1) provides a falsifiable, quantitative link between the model and measurable dialogue data. One can estimate the autocorrelation of semantic embedding sequences extracted from turn‑by‑turn interaction logs and fit an exponentially damped cosine; the fitted rate and frequency can then be compared with the predictions obtained by solving \det(\lambda I - A - B e^{-\lambda\tau}) = 0 with empirically estimated Jacobians.

---

6.2 Spectral signature of semantic 2‑cycles

In the super‑critical regime (\tau > \tau_c), the system settles onto a stable limit cycle. For the scalar prototype, the cycle is symmetric and, under the decoding d_{\text{probe}}(x) = \operatorname{sign}(x), corresponds to an alternation between two distinct semantic labels—a semantic 2‑cycle. The power spectrum of the semantic trajectory then exhibits a dominant peak at the Nyquist frequency (half the sampling rate) when the sampling is aligned with conversational turns. More generally, the Fourier transform of the paraphrase chain will display a sharp peak at the fundamental frequency \omega_{\text{cycle}} \approx 2\pi / T_{\text{cycle}}, where T_{\text{cycle}} is the period of the limit cycle in natural time units.

The occurrence of such spectral peaks is a robust, model‑independent indicator of oscillatory semantic dynamics. In the context of LLM paraphrasing, Wang et al. (2025) already observe convergence to a 2‑period state; our framework predicts that the emergence of this state is controlled by the effective feedback delay, and that its spectral signature should shift systematically as the interaction latency is varied.

---

6.3 Bifurcation threshold test

The most stringent empirical test of our theory is the systematic variation of the interaction delay \tau. If one conducts a controlled experiment in which the AI’s response latency is artificially increased (as in Tan et al. 2026) while measuring a scalar index of semantic coherence—such as the mean cosine similarity between successive turns, the entropy of the topic distribution, or the variance of the probing function—the model predicts a qualitative change at a critical delay \tau_c:

· For \tau < \tau_c, coherence is high, and the system converges quickly to a shared semantic frame.
· For \tau > \tau_c, coherence decreases, and the interaction exhibits persistent oscillation between alternative interpretations, i.e., fragmentation.

The critical delay \tau_c itself can be estimated from the linearised dynamics of the interaction, providing a parameter‑free prediction once the local Jacobians A and B are estimated from a baseline (low‑latency) condition. A failure to observe a bifurcation, or a bifurcation of opposite type (subcritical), would falsify the model as specified.

---

6.4 Falsifiability summary

We reiterate that, unlike the general free‑energy principle, our specific DDE model yields a clear set of falsifiable predictions:

1. The existence of a critical delay \tau_c beyond which semantic oscillations appear;
2. The supercritical nature of the bifurcation (soft onset of a stable cycle, no hysteresis);
3. The autocorrelation form (6.1) in the stable regime;
4. The characteristic spectral peak at the cycle frequency in the oscillatory regime;
5. The additive contribution of AI latency and human reflection to the effective delay.

If any of these predictions is contradicted by controlled experimental data, the model—not the underlying FEP—is wrong. This demarcation is essential for the theory’s scientific status.


Chapter 7: Summary of Contributions and Future Work


7.1 Summary of contributions

This thesis provides the continuous‑time DDE theoretical foundation for the discrete attractors observed empirically in LLM paraphrasing chains (Tacheny 2025; Wang et al. 2025). Our specific contributions are:

1. DDE framework for coupled human–AI semantics. We construct a delayed differential equation model on a low‑dimensional semantic manifold \mathcal{M}, with state vector x(t) = (x_P, x_C, x_S) and dynamics given by (2.1), rooted in the free‑energy principle and interactive repair.
2. Domain restriction to contextual hidden‑state manifolds. Following Mabrok (2026), we restrict analysis to the Riemannian submanifold of intermediate transformer hidden states, thereby addressing the manifold‑hypothesis criticisms raised by Robinson, Dey & Chiang (2025).
3. Novel repair functional G. Motivated by the clarification‑request phenomenology of Madureira & Schlangen (2023) and grounded in Clark’s (1996) Common Ground theory, we propose the first continuous‑time model of discursive repair as a direction‑specific force on the joint state manifold. The functional is fully specified with coupling strength \gamma, attention kernel K, and repair direction vector \mathbf{v}.
4. Rigorous stability and bifurcation analysis. We derive sufficient and necessary stability criteria (Lemma 2.1), prove the existence of robust semantic fixed points for small delay (Theorem 3.1), and compute the first Lyapunov coefficient for a scalar prototype in closed form (Theorem 4.1). The Lyapunov coefficient is shown to be strictly negative, establishing a supercritical Hopf bifurcation that gives rise to a stable semantic 2‑cycle—a direct theoretical counterpart to the observed period‑2 attractors.
5. Numerical validation. The analytical Lyapunov coefficient is validated with the bifurcation‑analysis package DDE‑BIFTOOL (Engelborghs, Luzyanina & Roose 2002), and the structural prediction is independently confirmed via the Wright‑type criterion of Balázs & Röst (2020).
6. Empirical anchoring and falsifiable predictions. We link the model’s delay parameter to real latency measurements (Tan et al. 2026), map the sub‑ and super‑critical regimes to observed human–AI storytelling dynamics (Fundal et al. 2025), and derive quantitative predictions for autocorrelation, spectral signatures, and the bifurcation threshold that can be tested in future experiments.
7. Delimitation from FEP unfalsifiability. By embedding the general free‑energy minimisation into a specific DDE with falsifiable consequences, we distinguish our theory from the often‑criticised unfalsifiability of the FEP itself.

---

7.2 Future work

Several extensions and open problems emerge from this investigation.

State‑dependent delays. The present analysis assumes a constant repair delay \tau. In reality, the delay may depend on the semantic distance: larger misalignments might trigger faster repair, leading to a state‑dependent DDE \dot{x} = F(x(t), x(t - \tau(x(t)))). The bifurcation theory for such equations is more involved (Walther 2003) but could yield a more accurate description of adaptive repair.

Full system centre‑manifold reduction. The Lyapunov coefficient has been computed only for the scalar prototype. A full centre‑manifold reduction of the coupled system (3.1) would yield a parameter‑dependent l_1 expressed in terms of \gamma, L_d, and the local geometry of V. This would allow quantitative predictions of whether a given human–AI dyad will exhibit super‑ or sub‑critical fragmentation.

Stochastic extensions. Conversational interaction is inherently noisy. Adding multiplicative or additive noise to (2.1) yields a stochastic DDE. In such systems, the bifurcation structure may be smeared or shifted; noise‑induced transitions could produce intermittent semantic oscillations even below the deterministic \tau_c. A stochastic analysis would connect the theory more closely to the variability observed in real dialogue.

Experimental validation. The predicted critical delay \tau_c should be tested in controlled human–AI interaction studies where the AI’s response latency is systematically varied and a scalar measure of semantic coherence is tracked. The experimental paradigm of Tan et al. (2026) already provides a template; adding a continuous measure of semantic alignment across turns (e.g., embedding autocorrelation) would directly test the predictions of Chapter 6.

Multi‑agent extensions. The present model couples a single human with a single AI. Extending the framework to multi‑party conversation—human–human–AI, human–AI–AI, or larger groups—would require multiple repair terms with distinct delay vectors and coupling topologies. The resulting dynamics could exhibit richer phenomena, such as partial synchronisation, clustering, or itinerant语义 switching, all of which are testable in group interaction data.


