Appendix A: Mathematical Foundations of Delay Differential Equations

This appendix collects the necessary background on delay differential equations (DDEs) used throughout the thesis. Standard references are Hale & Verduyn Lunel (1993) and Diekmann et al. (1995).

A.1 Phase space and solution operators

Consider the autonomous DDE with a single constant delay \tau > 0:

\dot{x}(t) = F(x(t), x(t-\tau)), \tag{A.1}

where x(t) \in \mathbb{R}^n and F: \mathbb{R}^n \times \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^n is C^k (k \ge 2). The state at time t is not simply x(t); one needs the history segment x_t: [-\tau, 0] \to \mathbb{R}^n defined by x_t(\theta) = x(t+\theta). The phase space is the Banach space C := C([-\tau,0], \mathbb{R}^n) equipped with the supremum norm \|\phi\| = \sup_{\theta \in [-\tau,0]} |\phi(\theta)|.

For a given initial function \phi \in C, there exists a unique solution x(t; \phi) defined on a maximal interval [-\tau, t_{\max}). The solution operator (semiflow) \Phi_t: C \to C is given by \Phi_t(\phi) = x_t(\cdot; \phi), where x_0 = \phi. The family \{\Phi_t\}_{t \ge 0} forms a C^k semiflow on C.

A.2 Linearisation and the infinitesimal generator

Suppose x^* is an equilibrium, i.e., F(x^*, x^*) = 0. Linearising (A.1) about x^* yields

\dot{y}(t) = A y(t) + B y(t-\tau), \tag{A.2}

where A = D_1 F(x^*, x^*) and B = D_2 F(x^*, x^*). The linearised semiflow on C is generated by the infinitesimal generator \mathcal{A}: \mathcal{D}(\mathcal{A}) \subset C \to C defined by

\mathcal{A} \phi = \dot{\phi}, \qquad \mathcal{D}(\mathcal{A}) = \{ \phi \in C^1([-\tau,0], \mathbb{R}^n) : \dot{\phi}(0) = A\phi(0) + B\phi(-\tau) \}.

The spectrum of \mathcal{A} consists of eigenvalues, which are the roots of the characteristic equation

\det(\lambda I - A - B e^{-\lambda\tau}) = 0. \tag{A.3}

Each eigenvalue \lambda has finite multiplicity, and the corresponding eigenfunction is \phi(\theta) = e^{\lambda\theta}v, where v \in \mathbb{C}^n satisfies (\lambda I - A - B e^{-\lambda\tau})v = 0. The spectrum contains only eigenvalues; there is no essential spectrum (Hale & Verduyn Lunel 1993, Ch. 7).

A.3 Stability theorem

The equilibrium x^* of (A.1) is (locally) asymptotically stable if and only if all roots of (A.3) have negative real parts. If at least one root has positive real part, x^* is unstable. At a stability boundary, roots cross the imaginary axis.

A.4 Bilinear form and adjoint theory

To project the flow onto centre manifolds, one needs a bilinear pairing between C and its dual space C^* = C([0,\tau], \mathbb{R}^{n*}) associated with the formal adjoint equation. For the linear system (A.2), the adjoint equation is

\dot{z}(s) = -z(s)A - z(s+\tau)B, \qquad s \in [-\tau,0],

where z(s) is a row vector. Equivalently, the transposed system on C^* is defined via the bilinear form

\langle \psi, \phi \rangle = \psi(0)\phi(0) + \int_{-\tau}^{0} \psi(\xi+\tau) B \phi(\xi) \, d\xi, \tag{A.4}

for \phi \in C and \psi \in C^*. This form is nondegenerate and can be used to decompose C into spectral subspaces. If \Lambda is a finite set of eigenvalues of \mathcal{A}, the corresponding spectral subspace P_\Lambda is paired with the adjoint spectral subspace P_\Lambda^* via \langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle, and the phase space splits as C = P_\Lambda \oplus Q, where Q is the orthogonal complement under the pairing.

A.5 Centre manifold theorem for DDEs

Assume that at a critical parameter value, the generator \mathcal{A} has a finite-dimensional centre subspace P_c corresponding to eigenvalues on the imaginary axis (typically a pair of purely imaginary roots \pm i\omega). The remaining spectrum lies in the left half-plane, bounded away from the axis. Then there exists a locally invariant, C^k-smooth centre manifold \mathcal{W}^c tangent to P_c at the equilibrium. The flow on the centre manifold is given by an ordinary differential equation in the centre coordinates, and the long-term dynamics near the bifurcation are governed by this reduced ODE.

The standard reference for centre manifold reduction in DDEs is Faria & Magalhães (1995). We outline their method: one writes the solution as \phi = \Phi z + \bar{\Phi} \bar{z} + w, where z are complex coordinates on the centre subspace, \Phi is a basis of eigenfunctions, and w belongs to the complementary subspace. Substituting into the DDE and applying the bilinear form yields a normal form ODE for z up to any desired order. The coefficients of the nonlinear terms depend on the original nonlinearity and the normalised adjoint basis.

