Appendix A: Mathematical Foundations of Delay Differential Equations

This appendix provides the necessary background on delay differential equations (DDEs) used throughout the thesis. The primary references are Hale & Verduyn Lunel (1993), Diekmann et al. (1995), and Smith (2011).

A.1 Phase space and semiflow

Consider the autonomous DDE with a single constant delay \tau > 0:

\dot{x}(t) = F(x(t), x(t-\tau)), \tag{A.1}

where x(t) \in \mathbb{R}^n and F: \mathbb{R}^n \times \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^n is C^k (k \ge 2). To obtain a well-posed initial value problem, one must specify the history of the state over an interval of length \tau. The natural phase space is the Banach space C := C([-\tau,0], \mathbb{R}^n) of continuous functions from [-\tau,0] into \mathbb{R}^n, equipped with the supremum norm \|\phi\| = \sup_{\theta \in [-\tau,0]} |\phi(\theta)|.

For any initial function \phi \in C, there exists a unique solution x(t; \phi) defined on a maximal interval [-\tau, t_{\max}). The segment of the solution at time t is denoted by x_t \in C, where x_t(\theta) = x(t+\theta) for \theta \in [-\tau,0]. The solution map \Phi_t: C \to C defined by \Phi_t(\phi) = x_t(\cdot; \phi) is a C^k semiflow: \Phi_0 = \text{id}, and \Phi_{t+s} = \Phi_t \circ \Phi_s for t,s \ge 0.

A.2 Linearisation and characteristic equation

Suppose x^* is an equilibrium point of (A.1), i.e., F(x^*, x^*) = 0. The linearised system about x^* is the linear DDE

\dot{y}(t) = A y(t) + B y(t-\tau), \tag{A.2}

where A = D_1 F(x^*, x^*) and B = D_2 F(x^*, x^*) are n \times n matrices. The linearised semiflow is strongly continuous, and its infinitesimal generator \mathcal{A}: \mathcal{D}(\mathcal{A}) \subset C \to C is given by

\mathcal{A}\phi = \dot{\phi}, \qquad \mathcal{D}(\mathcal{A}) = \{\phi \in C^1([-\tau,0], \mathbb{R}^n) : \dot{\phi}(0) = A\phi(0) + B\phi(-\tau)\}.

The spectrum of \mathcal{A} consists entirely of eigenvalues, which are the roots of the characteristic equation

\det(\lambda I - A - B e^{-\lambda\tau}) = 0. \tag{A.3}

For each eigenvalue \lambda, there is a corresponding eigenfunction \phi(\theta) = e^{\lambda\theta} v, where v \in \mathbb{C}^n satisfies (\lambda I - A - B e^{-\lambda\tau})v = 0.

Stability theorem: The equilibrium x^* is locally asymptotically stable if and only if all roots of (A.3) have negative real parts. If at least one root has a positive real part, the equilibrium is unstable.

A.3 Bilinear form and formal adjoint

To perform centre-manifold reductions, one needs a bilinear pairing between C and a dual space. Define the formal adjoint equation associated with (A.2) as

\dot{z}(s) = -z(s) A - z(s+\tau) B, \qquad s \in [-\tau,0],

where z(s) is a row vector. The phase space for the adjoint is C^* = C([0,\tau], \mathbb{R}^{n*}), the space of continuous row-vector-valued functions on [0,\tau]. A bilinear form \langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle: C^* \times C \to \mathbb{R} is defined by

\langle \psi, \phi \rangle = \psi(0)\phi(0) + \int_{-\tau}^{0} \psi(\xi+\tau) B \phi(\xi) \, d\xi. \tag{A.4}

This form is nondegenerate and allows the decomposition of C into spectral subspaces. If \Lambda is a finite set of eigenvalues of \mathcal{A}, the corresponding spectral subspace P_\Lambda and its adjoint P_\Lambda^* are paired via (A.4), and C = P_\Lambda \oplus Q with Q the orthogonal complement.

A.4 Centre manifold theorem

Assume that at a critical parameter value \tau = \tau_c, the generator \mathcal{A} has a finite-dimensional centre subspace P_c corresponding to eigenvalues on the imaginary axis (typically a pair \pm i\omega_c), while the remainder of the spectrum has strictly negative real parts. Then there exists a locally invariant C^k centre manifold \mathcal{W}^c tangent to P_c at x^*. The flow on \mathcal{W}^c is given by an ordinary differential equation in the centre coordinates, and the long-term dynamics near bifurcation are governed by this reduced ODE.

A constructive method for obtaining the normal form on the centre manifold was developed by Faria & Magalhães (1995). One writes the solution as

\phi = \Phi z + \bar{\Phi} \bar{z} + w,

where \Phi is a basis for P_c (typically the eigenfunction e^{i\omega\theta} and its conjugate), z \in \mathbb{C} is the centre coordinate, and w \in Q. Substituting into the DDE and using the bilinear form to project onto P_c yields an ODE for z up to any desired order.

