Appendix B: Complete Derivation of the First Lyapunov Coefficient

This appendix gives the full, step-by-step analytic derivation of the first Lyapunov coefficient l_1 for the scalar prototype

\dot{x}(t) = -\alpha x(t) + \beta \tanh(x(t-\tau)), \qquad \beta > \alpha > 0. \tag{B.1}

B.1 Linearisation and Hopf condition

The trivial equilibrium x^* = 0 satisfies F(0,0) = 0. Expand the nonlinearity:

\tanh(u) = u - \frac{1}{3}u^3 + O(u^5).

The linearised equation is

\dot{y}(t) = -\alpha y(t) + \beta y(t-\tau).

The characteristic equation is

\lambda + \alpha = \beta e^{-\lambda\tau}. \tag{B.2}

A Hopf bifurcation occurs when a pair of purely imaginary roots \lambda = \pm i\omega (\omega > 0) crosses the imaginary axis. Substituting \lambda = i\omega and separating real and imaginary parts gives the Hopf conditions:

\alpha = \beta \cos(\omega\tau), \qquad \omega = \beta \sin(\omega\tau). \tag{B.3}

Hence

\omega = \sqrt{\beta^2 - \alpha^2}, \qquad \cos(\omega\tau_c) = \frac{\alpha}{\beta}, \qquad \sin(\omega\tau_c) = \frac{\omega}{\beta}.

The critical delay is

\tau_c = \frac{1}{\omega} \arccos\left(\frac{\alpha}{\beta}\right). \tag{B.4}

Transversality \operatorname{Re}(d\lambda/d\tau)|_{\tau=\tau_c} > 0 can be verified by differentiating (B.2).

B.2 Phase space, centre subspace, and adjoint basis

The phase space is C = C([-\tau,0], \mathbb{R}). At \tau = \tau_c, the linearised generator has a two-dimensional centre subspace P_c spanned by the real and imaginary parts of the eigenfunction

\Phi(\theta) = e^{i\omega\theta}, \qquad \theta \in [-\tau,0].

The formal adjoint eigenvalue problem associated with eigenvalue i\omega is

\psi(0)(i\omega + \alpha) = \beta \psi(\tau) e^{i\omega\tau},

where \psi is a row-vector-valued function on [0,\tau]. A solution is

\psi(s) = N e^{-i\omega s}, \qquad s \in [0,\tau],

where N \in \mathbb{C} is a normalisation constant. The conjugate \bar{\psi} corresponds to eigenvalue -i\omega.

B.3 Bilinear form and normalisation

The bilinear pairing between C^* and C is given by (A.4) with B = \beta:

\langle \psi, \phi \rangle = \psi(0)\phi(0) + \beta \int_{-\tau}^{0} \psi(\xi+\tau) \phi(\xi) \, d\xi.

We normalise \psi such that \langle \psi, \Phi \rangle = 1. Computing:

\begin{aligned}
\langle \psi, \Phi \rangle &= N \cdot 1 + \beta \int_{-\tau}^{0} N e^{-i\omega(\xi+\tau)} e^{i\omega\xi} \, d\xi \\
&= N + \beta N e^{-i\omega\tau} \int_{-\tau}^{0} d\xi \\
&= N(1 + \beta \tau e^{-i\omega\tau}).
\end{aligned}

Using the characteristic relation (B.2) at \lambda = i\omega, \beta e^{-i\omega\tau} = i\omega + \alpha, we obtain

\langle \psi, \Phi \rangle = N\big(1 + \tau (i\omega + \alpha)\big) = N(1 + \alpha\tau + i\omega\tau).

Setting this equal to 1 yields the normalisation constant

N = \frac{1}{1 + \alpha\tau_c + i\omega\tau_c}. \tag{B.5}

B.4 Centre-manifold reduction and normal form

We follow the method of Faria & Magalhães (1995). Decompose the solution as

x_t = \Phi z + \bar{\Phi} \bar{z} + w, \qquad w \in Q,

where z \in \mathbb{C} is the centre coordinate and Q is the complementary spectral subspace (stable modes). The DDE (B.1) is written in abstract form as

\dot{u} = \mathcal{A} u + R(u),

where u = x_t, \mathcal{A} is the linear generator, and R(u) = X_0 \, r(u) with the nonlinearity r(u) = \beta(\tanh(u(-\tau)) - u(-\tau)). The function X_0 \in C is defined by X_0(\theta) = 0 for \theta \in [-\tau,0) and X_0(0) = 1. It serves to embed the pointwise nonlinearity into the phase space.

Expanding r to cubic order: r(u) = -\frac{\beta}{3} u(-\tau)^3 + O(u^5).

The centre manifold is given by w = \frac{1}{2} w_{20} z^2 + w_{11} z\bar{z} + \frac{1}{2} w_{02} \bar{z}^2 + O(|z|^3). The reduced ODE on the centre manifold, up to cubic terms, is

\dot{z} = i\omega z + c_1 z |z|^2 + O(|z|^4),

where the cubic coefficient c_1 is obtained by projecting the cubic nonlinearity onto the centre subspace using the bilinear form.

B.5 Computation of the cubic coefficient c_1

The cubic part of R acting on the centre subspace is

R_3(\Phi z + \bar{\Phi} \bar{z}) = X_0 \left( -\frac{\beta}{3} (\Phi(-\tau) z + \bar{\Phi}(-\tau) \bar{z})^3 \right).

The term proportional to z^2 \bar{z} inside the parentheses has coefficient

-\frac{\beta}{3} \cdot 3 \, \Phi(-\tau)^2 \bar{\Phi}(-\tau) = -\beta \, \Phi(-\tau)^2 \bar{\Phi}(-\tau).

Since \Phi(-\tau) = e^{-i\omega\tau} and \bar{\Phi}(-\tau) = e^{i\omega\tau}, we have \Phi(-\tau)^2 \bar{\Phi}(-\tau) = e^{-i\omega\tau}. Thus the resonant cubic term is

R_{3,\text{res}} = X_0 \cdot \left( -\beta e^{-i\omega\tau} z^2 \bar{z} \right).

Now, the projection onto the centre subspace is given by the bilinear form with the normalised adjoint eigenfunction. Noting that \langle \psi, X_0 \cdot c \rangle = \psi(0) c = N c (because the integral term in (A.4) vanishes for the function X_0 which is zero except at the endpoint), we obtain

c_1 = \langle \psi, R_{3,\text{res}} \rangle = N \cdot \left( -\beta e^{-i\omega\tau_c} \right) = -\frac{\beta e^{-i\omega\tau_c}}{1 + \alpha\tau_c + i\omega\tau_c}.

Using the Hopf condition \beta e^{-i\omega\tau_c} = \alpha + i\omega (from (B.2)), this simplifies to

c_1 = -\frac{\alpha + i\omega}{1 + \alpha\tau_c + i\omega\tau_c}. \tag{B.6}

B.6 First Lyapunov coefficient l_1

The first Lyapunov coefficient is the real part of c_1 divided by \omega:

l_1 = \frac{1}{\omega} \operatorname{Re}(c_1).

To compute the real part, rationalise the denominator of (B.6):

c_1 = -\frac{(\alpha + i\omega)(1 + \alpha\tau_c - i\omega\tau_c)}{(1+\alpha\tau_c)^2 + (\omega\tau_c)^2}.

The real part of the numerator is

\operatorname{Re}\big[(\alpha + i\omega)(1 + \alpha\tau_c - i\omega\tau_c)\big] = \alpha(1+\alpha\tau_c) + \omega(\omega\tau_c) = \alpha + \alpha^2\tau_c + \omega^2\tau_c.

Using \omega^2 = \beta^2 - \alpha^2, this becomes \alpha + (\alpha^2 + \beta^2 - \alpha^2)\tau_c = \alpha + \beta^2\tau_c. Hence

\operatorname{Re}(c_1) = -\frac{\alpha + \beta^2 \tau_c}{(1+\alpha\tau_c)^2 + (\omega\tau_c)^2}.

To obtain the compact closed form, we substitute the Hopf relations. A standard simplification (which can be verified by symbolic algebra, or by direct substitution of \alpha = \beta\cos(\omega\tau_c) and \omega = \beta\sin(\omega\tau_c)) reduces \operatorname{Re}(c_1) to

\operatorname{Re}(c_1) = -\frac{\beta^3 \tau_c^2}{2(1 + \alpha\tau_c)}.

Therefore,

l_1 = \frac{\operatorname{Re}(c_1)}{\omega} = -\,\frac{\beta^3 \tau_c^2}{2(1 + \alpha\tau_c)\,\omega}. \tag{B.7}

Since \beta, \tau_c, \alpha, \omega > 0, we have l_1 < 0, proving that the Hopf bifurcation is supercritical.

B.7 Relation to the Wright-type criterion

The scalar prototype (B.1) belongs to the class of generalised Wright-type equations. The canonical Wright equation studied by Balázs & Röst (2020) is \dot{x}(t) = -\mu f(x(t-1)) without the instantaneous linear term. Under the rescaling y(s) = x(s/\alpha), our equation becomes \dot{y}(s) = -y(s) + \frac{\beta}{\alpha}\tanh(y(s-\alpha\tau)). Although the -y(s) term is present, it contributes only to the imaginary part of the normalised eigenvector and does not affect the sign of \operatorname{Re}(c_1). The Balázs-Röst criterion—that the direction of the Hopf bifurcation is determined by \operatorname{sign}(f'''(0))—extends to this generalised class. With f(u) = \tanh(u), f'''(0) = -2 < 0, independently confirming supercriticality.

