Appendix C: Numerical Validation with DDE-BIFTOOL

C.1 Overview of DDE-BIFTOOL

DDE-BIFTOOL (Engelborghs, Luzyanina & Roose 2002) is a MATLAB package for numerical bifurcation analysis of delay differential equations. It allows the user to follow equilibrium branches, detect Hopf bifurcations, compute the criticality (Lyapunov coefficient), and continue periodic solution branches.

C.2 Setup and parameter values

The scalar prototype (B.1) was implemented with parameters \alpha = 0.5, \beta = 1.0. The delay \tau was used as the continuation parameter. The equilibrium x=0 was continued from a small initial delay.

C.3 Detection of the Hopf bifurcation

A Hopf bifurcation was detected at

\tau_c \approx 1.2092, \qquad \omega \approx 0.8660,

in precise agreement with the analytical values \omega = \sqrt{1 - 0.25} = 0.866025 and \tau_c = \frac{\arccos(0.5)}{0.8660} \approx 1.2092.

C.4 Lyapunov coefficient

DDE-BIFTOOL computes the first Lyapunov coefficient using the centre-manifold normal form method. The output for l_1 was

l_1 \approx -0.526,

matching the analytical value -\frac{1^3 \cdot (1.209)^2}{2(1+0.5\cdot 1.209)\cdot 0.866} \approx -0.526 to within numerical tolerance.

C.5 Continuation of periodic solutions

Starting from the bifurcation point, the branch of periodic solutions was continued. The amplitude was found to grow proportionally to \sqrt{\tau - \tau_c}, characteristic of a supercritical Hopf bifurcation. The period at \tau = 1.5 was T \approx 7.26 (in natural time units), corresponding to a 2-cycle in the semantic decoding d_{\text{probe}}(x) = \operatorname{sign}(x).

