Appendix D: Additional Proofs and Derivations

D.1 Proof of Lemma 2.1 (Stability criteria)

Part 1 (Matrix measure bound). The logarithmic norm (matrix measure) of A is defined as

\mu(A) = \lim_{h \to 0^+} \frac{\|I + hA\| - 1}{h}.

For any \lambda with \operatorname{Re}(\lambda) \ge 0, the resolvent bound \|(\lambda I - A)^{-1}\| \le -1/\mu(A) holds whenever \mu(A) < 0. If \|B\| < -\mu(A), then for all such \lambda,

\|(\lambda I - A)^{-1} B e^{-\lambda\tau}\| \le \|(\lambda I - A)^{-1}\| \, \|B\| \, |e^{-\lambda\tau}| \le \frac{\|B\|}{-\mu(A)} \cdot 1 < 1.

Thus the matrix I - (\lambda I - A)^{-1} B e^{-\lambda\tau} is nonsingular, implying \det(\lambda I - A - B e^{-\lambda\tau}) \ne 0. Consequently, no root can have \operatorname{Re}(\lambda) \ge 0, and the equilibrium is asymptotically stable for all \tau \ge 0 (the bound is independent of \tau).

Part 2 (Nyquist criterion). The characteristic equation can be rewritten as

\Delta(\lambda) := \det(I - (\lambda I - A)^{-1} B e^{-\lambda\tau}) = 0.

\Delta is analytic in \operatorname{Re}(\lambda) > -\delta for some \delta > 0. By the argument principle, the number of unstable roots equals the number of times the image of the Nyquist contour (the imaginary axis closed by a large semicircle in the right half-plane) winds around the origin. Stability is equivalent to the Nyquist plot \Delta(i\omega) (-\infty < \omega < \infty) not passing through the origin and having zero winding number. The condition stated in Lemma 2.1 is the non-vanishing of this determinant. ∎

D.2 Detailed proof of Theorem 3.1 (Semantic attractor)

Recall the statement and proof from Section 3.3, with the corrected uniform bound. Let x^* be a semantic fixed point with the stated properties. Local asymptotic stability implies there exist constants C, \sigma > 0 such that for all t \ge 0,

\|y(t) - x^*\| \le C e^{-\sigma t} \|y(0) - x^*\|.

The variance of the state satisfies \operatorname{Var}[y(t)] \le \|y(t)-x^*\|^2 \le C^2 e^{-2\sigma t} \|y(0)-x^*\|^2. Since d_{\text{probe}} is Lipschitz with constant L_p,

\operatorname{Var}[d_{\text{probe}}(y(t))] \le L_p^2 \operatorname{Var}[y(t)] \le L_p^2 C^2 e^{-2\sigma t} \|y(0)-x^*\|^2.

Now, for any \varepsilon > 0, choose \delta = \frac{\sqrt{\varepsilon}}{C L_p}. Then for any initial perturbation with \|y(0) - x^*\| < \delta,

\operatorname{Var}[d_{\text{probe}}(y(t))] \le L_p^2 C^2 \delta^2 e^{-2\sigma t} \le \varepsilon e^{-2\sigma t} \le \varepsilon

for all t \ge 0. This establishes Condition 4 of Definition 3.2. ∎

D.3 Dissipativity and global attractor (Hale 1988)

We impose the Lyapunov-type condition (4.5). The rigorous result is Theorem 3.4.6 of Hale (1988, Asymptotic Behavior of Dissipative Systems): If there exists a continuous function W: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^+ and constants c>0, M \ge 0 such that the derivative along solutions satisfies

\limsup_{h \to 0^+} \frac{1}{h}\big(W(x(t+h)) - W(x(t))\big) \le -c \|x(t)\|^2 + M,

then the system is point dissipative. For a DDE, one considers V(\phi) = W(\phi(0)) and the analogous condition on the semiflow. If, in addition, the semiflow is asymptotically smooth (which holds for DDEs with smooth F), then there exists a compact global attractor \mathcal{A}. Our condition (4.5) is the directional derivative version and ensures this property.

D.4 Multiple delays small-gain proof

Consider the characteristic equation (5.1):

\det(\lambda I - A - \sum_i B_i e^{-\lambda\tau_i}) = 0.

Suppose there exists a root \lambda with \operatorname{Re}(\lambda) \ge 0. Then |e^{-\lambda\tau_i}| \le 1 for all i. Rearranging, \det(I - (\lambda I - A)^{-1} \sum_i B_i e^{-\lambda\tau_i}) = 0 would imply that the matrix M = (\lambda I - A)^{-1} \sum_i B_i e^{-\lambda\tau_i} has an eigenvalue 1. Hence its spectral radius \rho(M) \ge 1. But

\rho(M) \le \|M\| \le \|(\lambda I - A)^{-1}\| \sum_i \|B_i\| \le \frac{\sum_i \|B_i\|}{-\mu(A)}.

The small-gain condition \sum_i \|B_i\| < -\mu(A) implies \rho(M) < 1, a contradiction. Hence no such unstable root exists, and the equilibrium is stable for all non-negative delays.

D.5 Additive delay approximation

For two delays \tau_1 \ll \tau_2 with comparable coupling strengths \beta_1 \approx \beta_2, the fast delayed term \beta_1 \tanh(x(t-\tau_1)) can be adiabatically approximated by its instantaneous equilibrium \beta_1 \tanh(x(t)) (since the fast variable relaxes quickly). The effective equation becomes \dot{x}(t) = -\alpha x(t) + \beta_2 \tanh(x(t-\tau_2)) + \beta_1 \tanh(x(t)). Linearising about zero, the linear terms combine to an effective instantaneous coefficient -(\alpha - \beta_1) and an effective delay \tau_2. For \beta_1 \approx \beta_2 and \alpha comparable, the bifurcation threshold for \tau_2 can be approximated as \tau_{c,\text{eff}} \approx \tau_c(\alpha_{\text{eff}}, \beta_2). The additive accumulation \tau_{\text{eff}} \approx \tau_1 + \tau_2 emerges in the limit where the fast delay is not too small but the spectral gap justifies the reduction; more rigorous justification follows from singular perturbation theory (Tikhonov’s theorem for delay equations).

